{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1955"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge nature/plants","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/nature__plants.g2.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",966],["+","The most widespread of them all is the first to flower. The Daffodil, Narcissus pseudonarcissus, described by Linnaeus in 1753 and known in German as „Osterglocke“ (Easter bell) or „Gelbe Narzisse“, contains lycorine and related alkaloids in every part, above all in the bulb. Daffodil bulbs are regularly mistaken for onions, and a bulb put into soup by mistake has sent schoolchildren to hospital with vomiting; the leaves should never be eaten either.\n\n"],["=",143],["+","\n| Daffodil | Narcissus pseudonarcissus | Osterglocke, Gelbe Narzisse | Lycorine |"],["=",52]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",888],["+","Die verbreitetste von allen blüht als erste. Die Osterglocke (Gelbe Narzisse), Narcissus pseudonarcissus, 1753 von Linné beschrieben, enthält in allen Teilen Lycorin und verwandte Alkaloide, am meisten in der Zwiebel. Narzissenzwiebeln werden immer wieder mit Speisezwiebeln verwechselt, und eine versehentlich in die Suppe geratene Zwiebel hat schon Schulkinder mit Erbrechen ins Krankenhaus gebracht; auch die Blätter dürfen nie gegessen werden.\n\n"],["=",125],["+","\n| Osterglocke | Narcissus pseudonarcissus | Gelbe Narzisse | Lycorin |"],["=",42]]}]},
